{
  "dom_id": "step-q112961",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+10,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{10}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 10, & \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 10.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)",
